<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip I, Count of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1143–<a href="/years/1191">1191</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Philip of Alsace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> was Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1168 to 1191, one of the most powerful princes of his age. He promoted Flemish towns and trade, held Vermandois through his wife Elisabeth, and acted as guardian of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>, but lost much of Vermandois to him by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-boves">Treaty of Boves</a> in 1185. He died at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Philip I, Count of Flanders" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Germain Demay · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigillum_Philippi_Comitis_Flandrie_1170.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
